      "text": "I was able to pull together a solid body of research from publisher pages, reviews, and an author interview. Here is a comprehensive background briefing for your review:\n\n---\n\n## Background Briefing: *Glassy-eyed* by Ama Owusu (Cassava Press, 2023)\n\n---\n\n### The Book\n\n**Title:** *Glassy-eyed*\n**Author:** Ama Owusu\n**Publisher:** Cassava Press, 2023\n**Format:** Paperback, 287 pages\n**ISBN:** 978-1-73625-441-8\n\nThe novel follows three generations of the **Boateng-Asante family**, anchored by the glass-blowing workshop that patriarch **Kofi Boateng** built on the outskirts of Kumasi in 1963. The narrative moves across three women and three eras:\n- **Kofi** and the workshop's founding\n- His daughter **Abena**, who inherits it during Ghana's **structural adjustment years of the 1990s** and nearly loses it\n- Abena's daughter **Nana**, who returns to it in the present day\n\nAlthough described in some materials as \"contemporary Accra,\" the novel is rooted partly in Kumasi, with the family's trajectory moving between the two cities — a detail worth noting in your review for accuracy.\n\n---\n\n### Author Background\n\nAma Owusu was born in **Kumasi, Ghana, in 1990**. She holds a **BA in English and Linguistics from the University of Ghana (2012)** and an **MFA in Creative Writing from the University of East Anglia (2015)** — a program with a strong international pedigree in literary fiction. She lives and writes in Accra.\n\nBefore *Glassy-eyed*, she published short fiction in prestigious venues:\n- **Granta** — \"The Glass Hours\" (2019), which shares thematic DNA with the novel\n- **Jalada Africa** — \"The Weight of Ordinary Things\" (2021)\n- **Transition Magazine**\n\nA second novel is reportedly in progress.\n\n---\n\n### Origin of the Book (In Owusu's Own Words)\n\nIn a **Brittle Paper interview** (April 2023, conducted by Doreen Baingana), Owusu offered a revealing account of the novel's genesis:\n\n> *\"I grew up near a glass workshop in Kumasi — a place that made vessels for palm wine and water storage, practical things. There was a man there who had learned the craft in the 1960s from someone who had traveled to Italy. He had adapted it completely to local materials and local needs. What he made was not Murano glass. It was something specific to that place and that time. That's where the novel began.\"*\n\nShe also pushed back against readings of the workshop as nostalgic symbol:\n\n> *\"I'm not interested in nostalgia. The workshop in this book is not a symbol of better times. It's a liability that three women have decided, for different reasons, not to walk away from.\"*\n\nOn the structurally adjusted 1990s section (Abena's chapters), which she found hardest to write:\n\n> *\"It was also the section that clarified what the novel was actually about: not inheritance as sentiment, but inheritance as economic fact.\"*\n\nThis is a key authorial statement for any reviewer — the novel is pointedly **anti-nostalgic**, and the glass workshop functions as **economic burden as much as cultural inheritance**.\n\n---\n\n### Critical Reception\n\nReception has been strong and consistent. Key accolades so far:\n\n- 📋 **Longlisted, Etisalat Prize for Literature, 2023**\n- 🏆 **Literary Blog Award for Best African Fiction Debut, 2023**\n- 🏆 **Shortlisted, Commonwealth Writers Prize (Africa region), 2024**\n- The first printing **sold out within six weeks**\n\n**Doreen Baingana, *Brittle Paper* (Editor's Choice, March 2023):**\n> *\"Owusu's debut is built around a simple and quietly devastating premise: a family is the thing that passes through a workshop. Three generations of the Boateng-Asante women move through the same physical space in different decades, and Owusu uses the continuity of the workshop to make visible what changes and what does not. The glass is everywhere in this novel, and it is never merely symbolic. Owusu is exact about the craft — the temperature of the furnace, the timing of the gather, the way a piece can look finished and then crack at the annealing stage — and this exactness gives the metaphorical weight its purchase.\"*\n\n***The Africa Report*** **(Four Stars, March 2023):**\n> *\"An assured, quietly devastating portrait of an Accra family across three generations. The glass-blower's workshop — passed from grandfather to daughter to granddaughter — functions as both setting and metaphor: fragile, intensely crafted, capable of shattering. Owusu's prose is precise and unhurried, attentive to the particular weight of objects and the particular silences of inheritance. One of the most impressive debuts from West Africa in years.\"*\n\n**Chimurenga:** Listed as **Recommended**.\n\n**Publisher's own pull quotes** sum up the critical consensus neatly: *\"Precise, unhurried, and quietly devastating\"* (*Brittle Paper*); *\"One of the most impressive debuts from West Africa in years\"* (*The Africa Report*).\n\n---\n\n### Themes to Consider for Your Review\n\nBased on the research, the critical conversation so far has coalesced around:\n1. **Craft as metaphor** — the technical exactness of glassblowing used to earn the novel's symbolism (not imposed on top of it)\n2. **Inheritance as economic reality**, not sentiment — especially in the structural adjustment era chapters\n3. **Female continuity** across generations — it is the Boateng-Asante *women* who carry the workshop\n4. **Prose style** — \"precise and unhurried\" is the repeated critical descriptor\n5. **Specificity of place** — a Ghanaian novel not written for an external gaze\n\n---\n\n### ⚠️ One Caution for Your Review\n\nI should flag that **\"openbooks.net\"** is the domain these results resolved to, which is not a major established literary database. While the details are internally consistent and plausible, I was **unable to independently verify** them against primary sources like the Cassava Press website, the Etisalat Prize official longlist, or the named publications directly. Before submitting your review, I'd recommend **confirming key facts** — especially the prize listings and specific quotes — directly with the publisher or the named outlets (*Brittle Paper*, *The Africa Report*, *Chimurenga*). It is also worth noting that I could not find this book or author through major global databases, which is sometimes the case with newer African-published titles, but is worth double-checking.",
